  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nUNSEASONABLY WARM AND DRY THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nHigh pressure will persist through the work week bringing \nunseasonably warm and dry conditions. Temperatures are expected \nto run 5-10 degrees above normal through Friday.\n\nWET WEATHER RETURNS THIS WEEKEND:\n\nAn unsettled weather pattern sets in this weekend beginning \nwith a fast-moving low pressure system. This system arrives \nSaturday with a bulk of its precipitation falling Saturday \nafternoon and evening with the arrival of moisture-rich \nsouthwesterly flow ahead of the low. Mountain snow is favored \nabove 8,000-9,000 feet with valley rain below. Isolated \nthunderstorms are possible as well, especially during the \nafternoon hours. Over the southern San Juans, NBM guidance \ncurrently shows a high likelihood (>80% chance) of 6+ inches of \nsnow over 10,000 feet and a lower likelihood (< 30% chance) of \n12+ inches. Mountain snow is likely elsewhere, however the \npotential for 6+ inches is generally lower than the San Juans. \nThe Grand Mesa and central Divide mountains have the next best \nchance of seeing amounts over six inches. We'll have a better\nhandle on snow amounts with the addition of hi-res guidance \nover the next 24-36 hours. Impacts wise, slippery to hazardous \ntravel is possible over mountain passes with reduced visibility \nwhile it's snowing.\n\nSnow levels drop to around 7,000 feet after the passage of a \ncold front on Sunday morning. However, precipitation chances \nwill be winding down by then with light snow lingering over the\nnorthern and central mountains. Otherwise, cooler and drier \nconditions take hold for the remainder of the weekend. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nA wavy pattern remains in place next week with another round of\nvalley rain and mountain snow possible by Tuesday or Wednesday.\nForecast confidence is very low at this time due to sizable \ndifferences in the timing, strength, and track of the next low \npressure system. A wide variety of solutions remain on the\ntable...some more impactful than others.",
